\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Steady To Up Late; \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fear Cap \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 11:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- -\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and some profit-taking helped curb the strength of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday as worries that Japanese authorities would step in to check the dollar's decline helped support the beleaguered U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But with sentiment on the dollar remaining bearish, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly steady to higher late in Asia, with the South Korean won chalking up a fresh six-month closing high - though off its intraday peak - in spite of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by government officials. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at KRW1,175.4 - its weakest close since July 25, 2002 - after trading between KRW1,171.0 and KRW1,176.8. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at KRW1,176.7 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar firmed to a new two-month closing high amid modest inflows of foreign funds into the stock market, and offers from exporters and interbank players. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said bids for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and importers pulled it back from its intraday high. The dollar ended at NT$34.490, from NT$34.528 previously, after trading between NT$34.450 and NT$34.502. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened slightly within a tight trading range. The Philippine peso was also lower while the Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah were little changed. \par \par \uc2  "It's still a weak dollar (story). The dollar has a lot of structural deficiencies," said Philip Wee, market strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. With the U.S. budget and current account deficits, and the specter of a war in Iraq, "I can't see how it (the weak dollar picture) can change at this point," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly dropped to a four-month low of Y117.55 overnight amid growing concerns about an imminent showdown in Iraq. The dollar also slid to its weakest level in more than three years versus the euro. \par \par \uc2  As of 0933 GMT, the dollar was quoted around Y118.14, up from Y118.06 late Tuesday in New York, but weakening from Y118.75 in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's new vice finance minister for international affairs, continued to warn markets the ministry is ready to act if needed to curb rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  "Excessive (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) movements are unwelcome because they are bad for Japan's economy," Mizoguchi told reporters on his first full day in his new position. \par \par \uc2  Later, during a two-day visit to Australia, Japan's economy and banking minister Heizo Takenaka told reporters during a two-day visit he was carefully watching developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but said it was up to the market to determine the correct level for the yen. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, an official at South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes the won is appreciating too quickly but it won't make an official comment on whether, or when, it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "We are monitoring the market...and the won's appreciation does seem a bit fast," the official said. \par \par \uc2  His comments followed remarks to Dow Jones Newswires by an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy earlier Wednesday that market participants shouldn't "underestimate the (South Korean) government's will to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  Kwon Tae-shin, director general of the ministry's International Finance Bureau, wouldn't discuss when or how the government might act to stabilize the market. \par \par \uc2  "If we think there are speculative movements, then we should do some kind of smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the remarks from the officials didn't have affect trading very much. \par \par \uc2  "It (the verbal warning) comes out everyday, so I don't think it has a big impact on the market," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  Still, in the near term, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep market participants from pushing the dollar below KRW1,170, a support level that has held for the past six months. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody is a little bit scared of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer. "They're very cautious and the dollar will keep trading between KRW1,170 and KRW1,175." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, traders said some agent banks of the Bank of Thailand were in the market when the dollar fell below 42.60 baht earlier in the day, though it was unclear if they were acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  An offshore bank dealer said the agent banks were likely trading for themselves. \par \par \uc2  "There's no real proof it's on behalf of the BOT. The BOT only comes in when the market is very volatile," he said. \par \par \uc2  There was also some importer buying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was quoted around THB42.65 late in Asia, from THB42.63 previously. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed was flat at 8,875 rupiah for the second straight day as dollar short covering erased all the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earlier gains. Intraday, the dollar traded between IDR8,860 and IDR8,880. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was modestly higher at S1.7348 late in Asia, from S$1.7335 Tuesday. During the Asian session, it moved between S$1.7300 and S$1.7350. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also up against the peso, closing at PHP53.480 on the Philippine Dealing System, from PHP53.390 in the previous session. Intraday, it traded between PHP53.330 and PHP53.495. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030115dz1f000oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
