\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Regionals rally on weak dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2002\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rallied\par strongly on Monday as the dollar, weakened by economic woes and\par fears of war, fell to two-month lows against the yen despite\par verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese officials.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar, South Korean won, Taiwan dollar and\par Thai baht hit levels last seen in September, while the\par Indonesian rupiah traded at its strongest since before the Bali\par bombings of October 12, and the Philippine peso was also near a\par one-month high.\par     After the local market had closed, the Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par lowered rates by 0.25 percent -- half the size of last week's\par cut in the United States. The Taiwan move had been widely\par expected.\par     "A predominantly majors-inspired move with some markets\par helped by the recent softening in oil prices and positive\par equity flows," said David Simmonds, emerging markets strategist\par at Royal Bank of Scotland in Singapore, of Monday's regional\par gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Simmonds said with the trade-weighted value of the dollar\par easing, most players would be "comfortable in staying long Asia\par against the dollar."\par     The dollar hit 119.51 yen, it lowest since September 13, as\par the market paid little attention to efforts by Japan's top\par finance officials to talk the yen down. A widening U.S. current\par account deficit, a possible war with Iraq, and higher interest\par rates elsewhere have all eroded sentiment on the dollar.\par     Analysts said that ahead of the Federal Reserve chairman\par Alan Greenspan's testimony to a congressional committee on\par Wednesday and key economic data at the end of the week, the\par dollar was likely to stay defensive -- which would support\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Signs that the Fed's easing could be working are perhaps\par needed to trigger a period of pullback for the dollar, which is\par looking oversold though markets remain biased to sell dollars\par on any rallies," said Jimmy Koh Chew Teck, head of treasury at\par UOB Bank in Singapore.\par     The Singapore dollar touched 1.7544 per dollar, its\par strongest since September 10, before paring some of those\par gains.\par     BAHT FIRMS BEYOND 43.0/DLR\par     The Thai baht firmed to hit 42.92 per dollar, its highest\par since September 19, supported by speculation over a possible\par cut in key interest rate which could boost foreign investment\par in Thailand's stock market.\par     Responding to the aggressive U.S. interest rate cut, the\par Bank of Thailand last week brought forward its next policy\par meeting to November 19, from December 12. Analysts believe the\par move indicates BOT may cut its 2.0 percent 14-day repurchase\par rate.\par     Dealers said talk of dollar purchases by importers and the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to beef up its reserves limited the baht's gains,\par although speculators are looking at the 42.80 level as a\par target.\par     The Philippine peso touched a near one-week high of 52.77\par per dollar, and traders were talking of a possible move to\par 52.50, before news of further fiscal slippage saw it end\par onshore trade at 52.91.\par     Philippine Finance Secretary Jose Camacho said there were\par indications that government revenues in October had fallen\par short of target. A government source said on Sunday that the\par deficit for the month was expected to be close to 13 billion\par pesos, and 180 billion pesos for the first 10 months of 2002.\par     The Indonesian rupiah briefly touched 8,955 per dollar --\par where it was before the Bali bombings on October 12 sent it to\par six-month lows --  helped by dollar sales from local\par corporates.\par     Rupiah sentiment was also helped by Saturday's news that\par Malaysia's number two lender, Commerce Asset-Holding Bhd\par , has bought a 51 percent stake in Indonesian\par mid-sized lender PT Bank Niaga for US$115 million in cash.\par     The Korean won rose to its strongest since September 10,\par helped by the yen's strength, and at one point broke through\par the 1,200 per dollar level.\par  \par     The Taiwan dollar was visited levels last seen on September\par 13, driven by the yen and U.S. dollar sales from exporters.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S.       $0828 GMT\par  Change on the day\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.80       120.78         +0.82\par  Sing dlr      1.7584       1.7607         +0.13\par  Korean won   1200.30      1207.40         +0.59\par  Baht           42.99        43.09         +0.23\par  Peso           52.93        52.88         -0.09\par  Rupiah       9145.00      9195.00         +0.55\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.80       131.24         +9.55\par  Sing dlr      1.7584       1.8515         +5.29\par  Taiwan dlr    34.400       34.986         +1.70\par  Korean won   1200.30      1277.00         +6.39\par  Baht           42.99        44.16         +2.72\par  Peso           52.93        51.50         -2.70\par  Rupiah       9145.00     10350.0