\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Tumble down, baht at 6-month lows. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2002\par 11:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Oct 7 (Reuters) - The Thai baht fell to \par \par \uc2  six-month lows and the Singapore dollar closed in on 16-week \par \par \uc2  troughs on Monday as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were pummelled by worries \par \par \uc2  over oil prices and the lockout in U.S. west coast ports. \par \par \uc2      Stumbling stocks and a weak yen added to the pressure, \par \par \uc2  sending the Taiwan dollar to 6-=   month lows, the South Korean \par \par \uc2  won to its weakest in 16 weeks and the Philippine peso to fresh \par \par \uc2  14-month lows. \par \par \uc2      "Risk aversion, weakness in global equities and increasing \par \par \uc2  worries on oil prices are likely to continue to weigh on Asian \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Sameer Goel, market analyst at Bank of \par \par \uc2  America in Singapore. \par \par \uc2      World crude oil prices pushed past $30 a barrel after a \par \par \uc2  suspected terror attack on a tanker off Yemen. \par \par \uc2      Sunday's explosion on the French-flagged Limburg heightened \par \par \uc2  tensions surrounding the oil-rich Middle East, as the United \par \par \uc2  States presses for the removal of Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2      In the United States, talks to end a week-long management \par \par \uc2  lockout of west coast ports broke off without agreement late on \par \par \uc2  Sunday and without any schedule for the next round. \par \par \uc2      Some regional economists fear a prolonged lockout could not \par \par \uc2  only delay Asian exports, but losses to the U.S. economy - \par \par \uc2  estimated at $1 billion a day - would hurt a fragile U.S. \par \par \uc2  recovery just as Asia was gearing up for a surge in demand \par \par \uc2  ahead of the Christmas holiday shopping season. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yen slipped to a two-week low against \par \par \uc2  against the dollar, as the Nikkei stock average crumbled to a \par \par \uc2  fresh 19-year low, taking most Asian markets down with it. \par \par \uc2  BAHT SLIPS \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht slipped to 43.72, last seen on April 3, as \par \par \uc2  oil importers bought dollars. But fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar \par \par \uc2  sales, expected around 43.75, prompted some profit-taking. \par \par \uc2      Dealers in Singapore said they suspect the Bank of Thailand \par \par \uc2  did sell dollars beyond 43.60 last week to prop up the baht. \par \par \uc2      However, analysts believe any support would be temporary \par \par \uc2  and aimed at dampening volatility, given Thailand's dependence \par \par \uc2  on imported oil and its increased correlation to moves in \par \par \uc2  dollar/yen in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2      The Singapore dollar turned volatile after a quiet start \par \par \uc2  when it touched near one-week highs at 1.7795 per dollar. \par \par \uc2      Taking its cue from the yen, the Sing slipped to 1.7916, \par \par \uc2  just shy of the 16-week lows at 1.7925 it visited last week. \par \par \uc2      Dealers said the move gathered pace as stop-loss Sing \par \par \uc2  dollar sell orders were triggered beyond 1.7860. As the Sing \par \par \uc2  slipped past 1.7880, some locals were spotted covering short \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar positions they had taken. \par \par \uc2  Ahead of the release of advance third quarter gross \par \par \uc2  domestic product estimates, due on Thursday, dealers said the \par \par \uc2  Sing dollar could ease further. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended onshore trade at 52.90 per \par \par \uc2  dollar, its lowest since August 10, 2001, weighed by a surge of \par \par \uc2  dollar buying from local corporates and speculative dollar \par \par \uc2  demand from offshore. \par \par \uc2      Dealers in Manila said they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \par \par \uc2  in the market on Monday, including just before the local close. \par \par \uc2      However, the gloomy global outlook prompted Taiwan's \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to loosen its grip a little and let the Taiwan \par \par \uc2  dollar ease through 35.0 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \par \par \uc2  defended that level last week, trying to counter equity-related \par \par \uc2  outflows. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  touched a low of 35.089, last visited on March \par \par \uc2  21, before closing onshore trade at 35.069 as the market still \par \par \uc2  feared \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said talk of some dollar sales by the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed views in the market that the bank's \par \par \uc2  tolerance level has now shifted to around 35.10. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won ended onshore trade at 1,240.9, its lowest \par \par \uc2  since June 17, as market eyed more investment outflows by \par \par \uc2  foreign funds with the benchmark KOSPI down by over 3.5 \par \par \uc2  percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said given Korea's high dependence on \par \par \uc2  imported oil, corporates hedging against higher oil bills were \par \par \uc2  expected to keep the won under pressure. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking in dollars just ahead of 1,241.0, the June 17 \par \par \uc2  low, capped the downside, though dealers said any support would \par \par \uc2  be temporary. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S.       $0836 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     123.92       122.79         -0.91\par  Sing dlr      1.7909       1.7802         -0.60\par  Taiwan dlr    35.069       34.994         -0.21\par  Korean won   1240.60      1232.40         -0.66\par  Baht           43.75        43.49         -0.59\par  Peso           52.85        52.73         -0.24\par  Rupiah       9010.00      9010.00         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     123.92       131.24         +5.91\par  Sing dlr      1.7909       1.8515         +3.38\par  Taiwan dlr    35.069       34.986         -0.24\par  Korean won   1240.60      1277.00         +2.93\par  Baht           43.75        44.16         +0.94\par  Peso           52.85        51.50         -2.55\par  Rupiah       9010.00     10350.00        +14.87.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021007dya700jsd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
