\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower Late As Yen Weakens On Econ Concerns \b0\par\par\uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2002\par 12:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower in Asia late Friday tracking the yen's weakness as lingering concerns over Japan's economy helped to hold the otherwise weak U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a free fall, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 0915 GMT (5:15 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7493, up from S$1.7438 late in Asia Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A late afternoon rally pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as high as S$1.7508, breaking S$1.7500 psychological resistance for the first time this week. The U.S. dollar traded between S$1.7439 and S$1.7500 for the rest of the day. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rally against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by speculative buying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen in Asian trading, which was touched off by a mixure of fears that Tokyo stocks may open lower and rumors of stop-loss buy orders at Y118.80, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened in Asia at around S$1.7490, but was pressed down to about S$1.7440 mid morning. The pair hovered in a tight 10-point band between S$1.7449-S$1.7459 before speculative buying by a major local bank bid the U.S. dollar up, a local dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The general fear that a sharp fallout of the U.S. dollar against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may trigger the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is another factor that supported the U.S. dollar's rally, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has been on the bottom of the MAS trading band for two weeks. If it breaks out of it, we'll be talking about a new trading range. It also has to do with the sentiment," said James Malcolm, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary-policy tool in seeking to maintain brisk growth and low inflation. The Singapore dollar is guided within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  According to Malcolm, the current trading band is between S$1.7450-S$1.7880. \par \par \uc2  However, most traders continue to peg the U.S. dollar's near-term resistance at S$1.7500 in anticipation that a slew of negative factors will continue to weigh on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. employment data - due later in the global session and is generally expected to be weaker-than-expected - could send the U.S. stock markets south and spur another wave of U.S. dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  "If the (U.S.) dollar finishes off above today's high of Y118.60 in New York tonight, we can expect the Singapore dollar to open at a new trading level next week," said the dealer at a major foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to find support at S$1.7440 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mixed Friday as some tenors shed their early gains due to profit-taking, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds had opened on a stronger note before modest pre-weekend consolidation pared some of those gains, although the outlook for local bonds remains generally supportive, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  In a research report published Friday, JPMOrgan Chase Bank said bond-swap spreads are unlikely to stay low for much longer as interest rates come off their recent bottom. \par \par \uc2  "We recommend buying bonds against swaps, with the view that bond-swap spreads will gradually widen," JPMorgan said. \par \par \uc2  "We advise initially positioning at the short end, which is a more defensive trade, and moving to longer tenor swap spread wideners over the next couple of weeks," JPMorgan added. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was up one basis point at 1.22%, while the yield on five-year bonds was down two basis points at 2.35%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was also two basis points lower at 2.82%, while the yield on 10-year bonds was unchanged at 3.38%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was two basis points lower at 3.73%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers were mostly higher as\par they tracked some local bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The two-year offer rose four basis points to 1.41%, while the\par five-year swap offer rose one basis point to 2.50%. The 10-year offer\par was up two basis points at 3.39%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 198 basis points\par from 200 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly a little higher as\par liquidity tightening modestly ahead of the weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers and analysts attributed the tightening to banks seeking\par short-term funding in anticipation of stronger demand for home-loan\par packages, especially amid the ongoing rate war between banks in\par Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late  Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.8750% 1.0000%   0.8750% 1.0000%\par 3-Month           0.8125% 0.9375%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.0000% 1.1250%   0.9375% 1.0625%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020906dy96000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
