\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Sharply On Dlr Short-Covering \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Fox \par \uc2 989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2002\par 11:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar surged against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday as a runaway correction smashed through technical resistance levels and investors rushed to cover short positions. \par \par \uc2  "The focus has shifted (from the U.S. dollar's long-term current account-related problems) to the fact that equity weakness in the U.S. ripples out to Asian equities - that U.S. growth, if undermined, can hit Asian growth," UBS Warburg \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Bhanu Baweja told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts remain convinced that a long-term bear trend for the dollar will reassert itself due to the difficulties the U.S. is expected to have funding its current account deficit, as foreigners reduce purchases of U.S. assets from levels seen in recent years. \par \par \uc2  But the long-term trend counted for nothing Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We are not convinced that the U.S. dollar is out of the woods yet. However, we would not advise standing in front of a runaway train," United Overseas Bank's Treasury Research department said in its daily note. \par \par \uc2  The train went out of control after the dollar closed in Tokyo Friday at Y117.73 to the dollar. By late Friday in New York, the dollar had climbed to Y118.94. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were already in for a beating Monday, but the regional trendsetter slipped further. By 0922 GMT (5:22 a.m. EDT) the dollar was at Y119.32. \par \par \uc2  The magnitude of the dollar's recent correction against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the UOB team, but they don't expect a further recovery in the dollar unless U.S. equities markets can sustain some of the stability they saw late last week. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts said the dollar broke above medium-term downtrend lines on its charts against all East and Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the Japanese yen, signaling an end to a rally in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that began in April. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty helped Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover in the afternoon, however, as tests of long-term downtrend lines lie ahead and exporters sold dollars heavily to take advantage of attractive levels. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, the dollar ended Monday at KRW1,200, off a high of KRW1,209 but up from a close Friday at KRW1,190.4. \par \par \uc2  The won's corrective decline of KRW29.1 Friday and Monday comprised the biggest two-day loss since late March 2001. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was also substantially weaker, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed its role in recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the U.S. dollar Monday and slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.894, up from a previous close of NT$33.689. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect a test of NT$34.00 if the yen - a regional trend setter - continues to fall. \par \par \uc2  Dollar buying by Indonesian corporate debtors ahead of the end of the month reinforced the regional trend, pushing the U.S. unit to an intraday high of 9,280 rupiah from a close Friday at IDR9,150. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah came off lows to close at IDR9,225 to the dollar, however, on profit-taking and suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended at PHP51.53 to the dollar, up from PHP51.30 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the peso was battered by both the general sell-off in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations that the government will have to sharply increase its budget deficit target. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar came off an early intraday high of PHP51.93, however, on profit-taking by banks and selling by exporters. The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it wasn't in the market Monday, as it was useless to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when all regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were falling sharply. \par \par \uc2  Against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the U.S. dollar was quoted late Monday at S$1.776, up from S$1.7589 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The break of S$1.765 was a major bullish signal for technical chart analysts. \par \par \uc2  But dealers warned that the rise took place in thin volume, as investors in the export-dependent city state waited for clearer direction on the U.S. economy and equities markets. \par \par \uc2  An Asian bank dealer in Singapore said the U.S. unit's rise looks overdone, but if it holds above S$1.76 in London and New York overnight Monday, it's likely to test S$1.78 in Asia Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The baht finished at THB42.39 to the U.S. dollar, down from a close Friday of THB41.82. \par \par \uc2  Short-covering after the dollar broke decisively through a technical resistance level - at THB42, on a downtrend line from April - pushed the U.S. unit to an early intraday high of THB42.50, it's highest level since June 6. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said resistance was strong at the high, and some players took profits on the dollar's sharp gains when regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilized in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Heavy selling in Thai equities last week also hurt sentiment toward the baht Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. funds have been selling Asian equities to lock in profits on this year's gains. \par \par \uc2  But UBS Warburg's Baweja said the selling can't continue to be as strong a factor into the medium term. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg's core long-term view is that the U.S. dollar will gradually weaken, but that the U.S. economy won't perform too badly, Baweja said. \par \par \uc2  He expects a modest recovery in capacity expansion in the U.S. in the latter part of the year, which will help Asian exports and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar should weaken due to current account problems in the long term, however, because you are not going to see a substantial rebound in the U.S. stock market, he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020729dy7t000la\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
