\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen slowing, not halting,  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2002\par 10:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 19 (Reuters) - Asian central bankers may know the financial market maxim - never try to catch a falling knife - but that has not stopped them from buying the ailing dollar to keep a lid on gains in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As the U.S. dollar has plunged against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, central banks in Seoul, Taipei and Bangkok have stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Policymakers are typically tight lipped about their reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say it is more likely directed at slowing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains against the dollar than halting them. \par \par \uc2  "In all of these countries, and this goes for Japan as well it's not so much about levels, it's more about the pace of appreciation which could be damaging to business confidence," said James Malcolm, JP Morgan Chase's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "So what they (central banks) are trying to do is to hold back the market from getting carried away," he added. \par \par \uc2  The story in any case is about dollar weakness rather than regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been hobbled by doubts over the U.S. ability to fund a gapping current account deficit while Wall Street swoons to multi-year lows on accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL FLOWS \par \par \uc2  "So the real story has been the capital flows. The flight out of the dollar has been the flight into Asian assets," said Micheal Spencer, Deutsche Bank's chief economist in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  He said large enough external surpluses and strong capital inflows would continue to pressure regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  Malcolm said central banks would continue to offset some of that pressure but eventually; "they will begin to take their foot off the pedal a little bit." \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an almost daily presence in the market, foreign reserves rose to an all-time high of $148.242 billion at the end of June, representing an increase of $8.421 billion from the end of May. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar strengthened below 33 per dollar this week to its strongest levels in 14 months. It has risen by about six percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2  NOT TOO SCARED \par \par \uc2  Analysts said intermittent comments, contravening with the spirit of these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , confirm that Asian governments in general may not be too averse to some modest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains against the background of broad dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said on Sunday the baht could strengthen to levels beyond 40 per dollar, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its highest level in two years. \par \par \uc2  And the Bank of Korea's Governor Park Seung said the won might remain strong for a while and efforts to curb it would have limited impact. \par \par \uc2  Park's comments were quickly watered down by statements from other officials to deter the market reading too much into them. \par \par \uc2  NOT EXPENSIVE YET \par \par \uc2  Naomi Fink, UBS Warburg's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Singapore said the difference of opinion among finance officials, to some extent, stems from the uncertainty over the yen which has also soared in past weeks. \par \par \uc2  The yen has risen 15 percent against the dollar since early April despite bouts of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the uncertainty over where Japanese policymakers would put a cap on the yen has spread to the region," she said. \par \par \uc2  Although the correlation has withered in recent years, Asian policymakers keep a close track of the yen, as Japan is both a competitor and a major market for most of these economies. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost over 13 percent of its trade-weighted value since the start of the year, as it plunged recently to 17-month lows against the yen and around 2-= lows against the euro . \par \par \uc2  Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have followed suit, but analysts said they have underperformed the yen and euro, meaning trade-weighted gains have been moderate and impact on export competitiveness modest. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020719dy7j00g1t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
