\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Late;Mkt Watches NE Asia,Ignores MAS \b0\par\par\uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 11:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was little changed against its U.S. counterpart late Thursday after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was shaken and then revived by events in Northeast Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar surrendered early gains after reports of South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the U.S. dollar unsettled local players. However, late-session gains by the Japanese yen revived the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to late Wednesday's level. \par \par \uc2  The local dollar, however, didn't specifically react to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's widely anticipated decision to keep it's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -centered monetary policy in neutral gear to aid the Singapore economy's recovery. \par \par \uc2  At 0825 GMT (4:25 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7502, compared with S$1.7505 late Tuesday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7490 and S$1.7535 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened slightly higher around S$1.7513 in Asia but, after an initial lull in trading, drifted to an intraday low of S$1.7490 in line with its early losses against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Subsequently, following reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the South Korean government to cap the won's rise, the U.S. dollar reversed direction against its Singapore counterpart and rose to an intraday high of S$1.7535. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean government Thursday bought $200 million in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through state-run banks, in a bid to support the falling U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Yonhap Infomax newswire reported, citing traders. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's fell to around KRW1,172 early in the session, after closing Wednesday at a 19-month low of KRW1,179.4, after the Korean government indirectly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar's rally against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ran out of steam around midday and it subsequently retreated to trade in a tight S$1.7500-S$1.7510 range for most of the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's retreat against the Singapore dollar in the afternoon followed a fresh bout of weakness in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen. The U.S. dollar fell to in intraday low of Y117.33 from an early high of Y117.93. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar didn't react much to the MAS statement. However, analysts said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's neutral policy stance means that while the Singapore dollar will remain steady against its trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket and therefore continue to rise against the U.S. dollar in line with strength in the yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  The cyclical recovery in Singapore's economy and the bearish outlook for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could result in the U.S. dollar falling to S$1.7000 by the year-end. In late March, the U.S. dollar rose as high as S$1.8500. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the U.S. dollar appears to be consolidating around the S$1.7500 for now, and expect it to trade between S$1.7480 and S$1.7530 overnight in London and New York. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended higher Thursday, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys. Dealers said the MAS' semiannual monetary policy statement had a negligible impact on bond prices. \par \par \uc2  A local bank dealer noted that bond prices have mostly risen this week but Thursday's trade volume was smaller than that of recent sessions. That suggests market participants' appetite for bonds may be running out, which could result in profit-taking in coming sessions. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds fell three basis points to 1.37% while the yield on five-year bonds fell eight basis points to 2.73%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds fell eight basis points to 3.23%, while the yield on the 10-year bonds fell five basis points to 3.78%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds fell six basis points to 4.07%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interest rate swaps ended lower, tracking bond yields. The seven-year\par swap offer fell four basis points to 3.42%, while the 10-year swap offer\par ended three basis points lower at 3.82%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 221 basis points\par from 220 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates remained steady at low levels amid\par flush liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.3750% 0.5000%   0.3750% 0.4375%\par 3-Month           0.6250% 0.7500%   0.6875% 0.7500%\par 1-Yea