\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; BOJ Action Little Help To Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 11:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar's slide against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  picked up steam Monday as attempts by Japanese authorities to bolster the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen had little impact on the rest of the region. \par \par \uc2  Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained at multi-month highs amid growing investor distaste for the dollar, which has come under pressure from falling U.S. stock prices, Middle East tensions and the large U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Only the Philippine peso, hampered by concerns the government will overshoot its fiscal deficit target, fell against the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  did succeed in lifting the struggling dollar briefly from a seven-month low of around Y121 to a daily high of Y122.80. \par \par \uc2  Authorities bought an estimated $2 billion to $3 billion to prop up the dollar, marking their fifth market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a month. But within minutes market players pushed the dollar back down to Y122, and near the end of Asian trading it had slipped to Y121.30. \par \par \uc2  "The jump in dollar/yen was only momentary," said Naomi Fink, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While fears of further BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may make some players wary of pushing the dollar lower, the fact remains in such an environment of global weakness \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do little more than slow the pace of the dollar's decline, Fink said. \par \par \uc2  And most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to capitalize on the dollar's fragility, with the South Korean won, Thai baht and Singapore dollar poised to gain the most, she added. \par \par \uc2  The won gave up some of its gains Monday in tandem with the yen's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inspired fall, but still had enough momentum to finish at an 18-month high against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,213.5 won, below KRW1,219.4 Friday. It was the won's strongest finish since KRW1,209.2 on Dec. 19 2000. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had fallen as low as KRW1,210.7 in early trading before verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the South Korean government and the subsequent action by Japanese authorities pulled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  The won often tracks the yen because of the similarities between South Korean and Japanese exports and export markets. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht posted a 21-month high, raising concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into the market to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's broad weakness and improving prospects for the Thai economy fueled baht buying, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at THB41.800, down from THB41.990 late Friday. It was the baht's best performance since September 2000. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar powered to a 13-month high despite aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.772, compared with NT$33.855 Friday. It was the New Taiwan dollar's strongest close since NT$33.495 on May 25, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The market was full of U.S. dollar sellers, but there were few buyers. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked hard to keep the dollar bid, buying about $2 billion on a day where the volume of all transactions totaled $2.65 billion. \par \par \uc2  "It was mostly the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in there today," said one dealer at a foreign bank in Taipei, noting that both exporters and offshore players were selling the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar finished Asian trading at a nine-month high. Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.765, down from S$1.772 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the U.S. dollar's sharp fall, market participants said the move wouldn't likely draw the attention of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely to tolerate a strong Singapore dollar when other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are strengthening as well, they said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very often, but has done so in the past when the Singapore dollar had strayed outside of an undisclosed trading band that measures the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a trade-weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's recent slide has probably pushed the lower range of the trading band to S$1.7500, according to a report from United Overseas Bank. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah capitalized on regional dollar bearishness and positive sentiment surrounding the country's loan program with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at IDR8,595, down from IDR8,630 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The IMF late last week announced it was disbursing the next US$358 million of loans to Indonesia as part of a $5 billion assistance package. \par \par \uc2  However, the IMF warned that a recent government decision to declare bankrupt the local affiliate of Canadian insurer Manulife showed the country's legal system needs further reforms. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, lingering fiscal concerns and sluggish U.S. dollar inflows weighed on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.35 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP50.32 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The peso should be hovering just above PHP49 against the dollar, but the market is pricing in the budget deficit," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The government recently reported that its budget deficit reached PHP82.69 billion for the first four months of the year, a pace that would put it significantly over its full-year target. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020624dy6o000k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
