\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, Tracking Stronger Yen, Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2002\par 10:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was stronger Thursday, in tandem with a firmer yen, as well as other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as market participants trimmed long U.S. dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But trading volume was as thin as in the past few days, with little direct interest in the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  At 0840 GMT (4:40 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7836, down from S$1.7867 late Wednesday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7830 and S$1.7865. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7860, and came under moderate selling pressure as market participants in Asia continued to unwind long U.S. dollar positions in the wake of the overnight slide In U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar continued to drift lower, in tune with its falls against its other regional counterparts, in particular the yen, through the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers were quick to add that trade in the local market was thin, and has been so for the past few days, with the Singapore dollar generally taking its lead from the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to a session low of Y123.73 from its intraday high of Y124.22 in Asia, sparking a broader slide against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is jittery. It seems the U.S. economy is not as good as people had expected. So nobody wants to hold long (U.S.) dollars," a Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said lingering concerns over tensions in the Middle East as well as renewed fears of fresh terrorist activity in the U.S. worsened the already weak U.S. dollar sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to retreat further against the local dollar in overnight sessions in New York and London as market participants continue to trim their long positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  should keep the U.S. dollar supported around S$1.7820, with resistance at S$1.7880. \par \par \uc2  After rallying sharply since June 12, prices of Singapore government bonds surrendered early gains to end significantly lower Thursday as market participants locked in profits, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking had been anticipated after the recent sharp rally. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market participants were also keen to sell bonds ahead of a string of U.S. data to be issued later Thursday. These include data on the current account surplus in first quarter and jobless claims for the week of June 15. \par \par \uc2  "The selling was quite orderly and the volume moderate," a dealer at a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds rose four basis points to 1.40%, while the yield on five-year bonds rose 12 basis points to 2.71%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds rose nine basis points to 3.15% and the yield on 10-year bonds gained 12 basis points to end at 3.58%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds rose 11 basis points to 3.93%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps tracked local bond yields\par higher, with the seven-year swap offer rising eight basis points to\par 3.34% and the 10-year swap offer gaining 10 basis points to 3.69%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 199 basis points,\par from 198 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, term rates were mostly steady amid flush\par liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.7500% 0.6875%   0.7500% 0.6875%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8125%\par 1-Year            1.0000% 1.1250%   1.0000% 1.1250%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad and Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020620dy6k000il\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
