\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won Ends Dn On Ctrl Bk Talk \b0\par\par\uc2  By S. Anuradha \par \uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 13:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Friday, with comments by several central bankers in the region indicating different levels of comfort with the dollar's recent weakness. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won fell against the dollar as the market struggled to interpret remarks by the Bank of Korea, while the Thai baht finished firmer thanks to equities-related inflows and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reassurance it was comfortable with the baht's strength. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, Singapore dollar, Taiwanese dollar and peso were mostly flat late, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in the Philippines said he thought the peso should trade higher. \par \par \uc2  Friday's trade continued to be muted with many participants distracted by key World Cup matches, and volume in many markets was thin. \par \par \uc2  Early in the session, Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung said that the recent weakening in the dollar against the won has reduced the need for an interest rate hike in Korea, a comment some took to indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tolerate a stronger won. But Park also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to buy dollars in the market if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's drop versus the won is "excessive" - suggesting the bank won't let the won strengthen much more. \par \par \uc2  The market eventually chose to focus on the latter interpretation after a spate of afternoon dollar-buying by state-run banks spurred a round of dollar short-covering, pushing up the dollar to 1,236.1 won, above Wednesday's finish at KRW1,233.6. South Korean markets were closed Thursday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, the dollar dipped against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Moody's Investors Service announced a surprise upgrade of the nation's ratings. The dollar hit an intraday low of S$1.7835 after Moody's raised Singapore's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceilings for bonds and deposits, as well as the government's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating, to Aaa, its highest grade, from Aa1. \par \par \uc2  The ratings agency said that the upgrade reflects continued strengthening of the country's external financial position over the past several years. Although Singapore's fiscal surplus has declined recently, Moody's said the government has very large holdings of financial and other assets and no foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. dollar having retreated significantly from a high of S$1.7945 Monday, market participants were wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  These concerns prompted foreign and local banks to buy U.S. dollars, lifting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above S$1.7860. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at S$1.7865, unchanged from levels a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the dollar finished at 42.175 baht, down from THB42.265 Thursday. Capital inflows for foreigners' portfolio investments continued to weigh on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a Bangkok dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said late Friday he isn't concerned about the baht's strength. "There is nothing serious about the baht's recent strength. It moves up and down. That's normal," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The market wasn't affected much by the governor's comments, as they confirmed existing policy and were identical to comments of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials issued repeatedly in recent weeks as the baht has slowly strengthened. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Philippine peso closed flat amid a dearth of fresh incentives. The dollar closed at its intraday high of PHP50.470, little changed from PHP50.465 Thursday, as a recent rally by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be running out of steam. \par \par \uc2  The peso's downside was supported by comments from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, who said the dollar should fall below PHP50.00 given the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and expectations of a sizable balance-of-payments surplus this year. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should be below PHP50 and around PHP49," said \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the dollar to move between PHP50.15 and PHP50.50 in the coming week. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended stronger against its U.S. counterpart on U.S. dollar selling by exporters - although the rise was capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT (4:00 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.112, down from NT$34.121 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's U.S. dollar bids totaled around US$150 million. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah ended steady in range-bound trading, but is expected to head higher next week following gains in local stock prices in the last four sessions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,740 rupiah, little changed from its close at IDR8,745 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that dollar demand from local companies to pay maturing offshore debts offset dollar offers from foreign banks, leaving the dollar range-bound. \par \par \uc2  Local companies are saddled by around $60 billion of foreign debts, which they borrowed before the 1997-1998 Asian financial crisis. Most Indonesian companies only managed to get a three-year rollover on the payment of the debts, which means some of them started making repayments last year. Offshore debt payments occasionally weigh on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  -By S. Anuradha, Dow Jones Newswires; 9122-2884212; su.anuradha@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020614dy6e000pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
