\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Persist \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 11:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Concerns that Japanese monetary authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the dollar against the yen kept a lid on all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears were heightened after the Bank of Japan bought dollars against the yen Tuesday for the fourth time in two weeks in an attempt to control the yen's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ acted on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has expressed concern that excessive yen strength could undermine the country's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  While most analysts agree that no amount of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can reverse the negative sentiment toward the dollar in global markets, the $20 billion spent by the MOF so far has been successful in stinging market players who have taken short positions in the dollar against the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is afraid of more MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar is likely to stay supported for now, which will indirectly cap Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains," said Jimmy Koh, head of Treasury research at United Overseas Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen by about 7% against both the yen and euro since March as global investors have shifted capital away from the U.S. into markets in Europe and Asia looking for better returns. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall against its major counterparts, combined with an improving outlook for Asian economies, has pushed most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-month highs in recent trading sessions. \par \par \uc2  Following the dollar's bull run over the past few years, many analysts say U.S. government authorities would welcome a gradual weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it would help U.S. manufacturers become more competitive in global export markets. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at 124.30 yen, up from Y123.99 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $0.9374, down from $0.9395 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won backed off an 18-month high to finish the day weaker after officials from both the Bank of Korea and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy made public statements expressing their concern over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded as low as 1,218.7 won at midday as exporters sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively. The dollar bounced back after the jawboning to close at KRW1,224.3, up from KRW1,220.1 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Korea Governor Park Seung warned that the won's recent appreciation was hurting the competitiveness of South Korean exporters. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take unspecified measures to counter the won's appreciation against the dollar if the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise hurts the economy. \par \par \uc2  Echoing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's comments, an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said the government was "greatly concerned" about the negative impact on the economy from the won's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso came under pressure from the yen weakness and continuing concerns over a political dispute that has split in the Senate leadership. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.800 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP50.430 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The defection of a government-allied senator means the opposition now controls 12 of the upper house's 24 seats. There were concerns that the split could delay passage of key reform bills needed to push the economy forward. \par \par \uc2  "Jitters over the situation in the Senate and its impact on the government's economic agenda continue to hound the market," said a trader with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah fell sharply, triggering speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rally to a nine-month high may be near its end. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at IDR8,900, up from IDR8,650 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The decline "reaffirms our position that the risk-reward has turned against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the market over-stretching its optimism on Indonesian assets," Bank of America said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  A string of failures in the country's economic reform program in recent weeks, including last week's decision to cancel an auction of a 51% stake in Bank Niaga due to low bids, raised concerns about the sustainability of the rupiah rally, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar finished a touch weaker, affected only marginally by the yen's decline. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.097, compared with the previous close of NT$34.093. \par \par \uc2  "You saw the yen weaken a bit, but on the other hand most players still think that the Taiwan dollar has more room for appreciation, so the two factors balanced each other out," a foreign bank dealer in Taipei said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slipped as the decline in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted foreign and local banks to cover their short positions in the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7945, up from S$1.7836 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also followed other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, with a lack of domestic developments to drive trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 42.51 baht in late Asian trading, up from THB42.31 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020605dy65000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
