\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Higher Late;Only Peso, Rupiah Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2002\par 12:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Ongoing concerns about the U.S. economy and stock markets pushed the dollar lower against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Only the Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah failed to take advantage of the dollar's global weakness. \par \par \uc2  Most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to trade at multi-month highs, with the South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar posting their best performances in 17 months and one year respectively. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened the Asian trading day on the defensive, with overnight declines in U.S. equity markets underscoring the negative wave of sentiment weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The weakening bias in the U.S. dollar persists, with the new week kicking off in an increasingly familiar manner," BNP Paribas said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  Sizable declines in both the Dow Jones Industrial Average and the Nasdaq Composite Index overwhelmed a report from the Institute for Supply Management that showed the U.S. manufacturing sector grew robustly in May. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, a new round of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korean authorities briefly halted the won's rise, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its climb shortly thereafter as exporters resumed their dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,220.1 won, down from Monday's close at KRW1,226.3, and its lowest level since December 2000. \par \par \uc2  Concerned that the won was appreciating too rapidly, the Finance Ministry issued a statement that it was concerned about market "uneasiness" and would take necessary action. \par \par \uc2  "No one believes what the government says anymore," said a dealer at a local bank. "Unless there's some actual dollar purchase by the government, the market isn't likely to react." \par \par \uc2  The South Korean government has repeatedly threatened action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past three weeks, but has yet to take any concrete steps. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.093, down from NT$34.139 Monday. It was the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strongest finish since last June. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar's decline will likely slow as it reaches the NT$34.00 support level. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also expected to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it from falling to sharply. \par \par \uc2  The peso came under pressure following negative news regarding the country's budget deficit, and a brewing political storm in the government. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.430 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP50.360 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Reports that tax collections for May were lower than expected fueled ongoing concerns that the government may not be able to meet its budget deficit target for the year. \par \par \uc2  The government has already overshot its budget target for the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the peso also was dented by what many termed as a "senate coup" staged Monday, when opposition lawmakers wrested the leadership of the chamber from senators allied with President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo's government. \par \par \uc2  The defection of a government-allied senator has given the opposition control of 12 of the chamber's 24 seats. \par \par \uc2  "The market is concerned that such political events might backlash on the government's economic agenda," said a trader with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah weakened slightly after market participants booked profits following the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rally, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at IDR8,650, up from IDR8,540 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah rallied to nine month high Monday due to investor optimism regarding the country's debt restructuring effort, and economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tracked the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7836, down from S$1.7878 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The market has been reluctant to sell the U.S. dollar more aggressively for fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The baht also moved in tandem with its regional peers with little in the way of domestic news to drive trading. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 42.31 baht, down from THB42.38 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020604dy64000na\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
