\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late As US Dlr Falls Against Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2002\par 12:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was stronger late Tuesday amid in light volumes, as the U.S. dollar slipped against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0914 GMT (5:14 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7836, down from S$1.7878 late Monday. It traded between S$1.7830 and S$1.7850 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower in Asia, around S$1.7850, following its overnight decline against the yen. It then drifted down against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on selling by banks and investment houses. \par \par \uc2  Attempts by Japanese officials to talk down the yen failed to stem the dollar's fall against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan's authorities fear a stronger yen might hurt exports and damage the chances of an economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "On the whole the U.S. dollar is looking quite bearish, but we expect it to take a breather before moving much lower because players are wary that (regional) central banks may oppose such a move," an Asian bank dealer in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, in recent sessions Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to stem the U.S. dollar's slide, and in Singapore the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have bought U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this is a key reason why market participants have been reluctant to push the U.S. dollar sharply lower against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Charts show the U.S. dollar is nearing its maximum 61.8% Fibonacci support level at S$1.7768. This level was generated by the U.S. dollar's rally to S$1.8558 in late December from a low of S$1.7280 low in September. \par \par \uc2  While market participants fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the U.S. dollar's slide below S$1.7800, the MAS is expected to be more tolerant of a weaker U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of its recent losses against the euro, yen and sterling. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore quarterly survey published Tuesday showed economists on average expect the country's economy to grow by 2.4% on the year in the second quarter after a 1.7% shrinkage in the first. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ignored the survey findings, as such expectations had already been priced in. \par \par \uc2  Singapore government bonds ended mixed Tuesday, with prices of some tenors rising a touch after U.S. Treasurys gained overnight. The longer-dated tenors were flat to lower as market participants adjusted their portfolios ahead of a scheduled auction of 10-year bonds due latest this month. \par \par \uc2  The yields on two- and five-year bonds were both steady at 1.56% and 2.94%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was flat at 3.51% while the yield on 10-year bonds rose one basis point to 3.94%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds also rose one basis point, to 4.41%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer fell one basis point to 3.70% and the 10-year swap offer also\par slipped one basis point, to 4.06%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 217 basis points\par from 216 basis points late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were steady to a touch lower amid\par flush liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.6250% 0.7500%   0.6875% 0.8125%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.0625% 1.1875%   1.1250% 1.2500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4152;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020604dy64000la\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
