\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Mostly firm, but off early highs. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Ovais Subhani \par \uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2002\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, May 28 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were \par \par \uc2  firm late on Tuesday, aided by a steadier yen , but gains \par \par \uc2  were capped with the market wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and awaiting \par \par \uc2  consumer data from the United States. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Those concerns pulled the Korean won         -2 down to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1,237.5 per dollar at the close of onshore trade, from a fresh\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 17-month high of 1,225.5. The Taiwan dollar was also\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par off its new 11-month high of 34.275 to end onshore trade at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 34.309.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     While analysts said dollar losses could be limited by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par actual or feared \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the near-term\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par outlook for the greenback was bearish.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "Japanese stocks remain attractive to investors looking for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the next big thing, especially while the US market is not so\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par hot while the rest of Asia is booming," said Marshall Gittler,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par head of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Bank of America in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The dollar managed to stay almost a yen above last\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday's lows of around 123.50 when the Bank of Japan\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop it up, but down from late Monday's 124.86.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The dollar was also within sight of eight-month lows\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par against the euro .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     CAUTIOUS GAINS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Dealers in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said concerns over verbal and actual\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the yen and other North Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sending markets in Southeast into bouts of dollar short\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par covering, given that most players were extremely short of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Those concerns pulled the Singapore dollar off its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par early session high of around 1.7900, even after a surprise rise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in April manufacturing production data that confirmed a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par tentative rebound was gaining momentum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The first quarter business receipts index however shrank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1.7 percent on the year, larger than the previous quarter's 0.3\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent drop, showing the services sector may still lag.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The April manufacturing production index rose 8.1 percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year on year beating forecasts of minus one percent to plus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par three percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said Singapore dollar eased to levels around\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1.7940, as dollar bids emerged around 1.7920-1.7930 range early\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on. It was still higher from late Monday's 1.7083.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah stalled at around 8,845 per\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar, a 7-= month high, as the market awaits a final round\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of bidding for a majority stake in state-owned Bank Niaga\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Two out of four consortia were qualified on Monday to buy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the 51 percent stake, but authorities say their bids should\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par meet price expectations for the sale to proceed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The qualifying consortia are led by Australian and New\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Zealand Banking Group Ltd and Malaysia's second\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par largest lender, Commerce Asset-Holding Bhd .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Financial Sector Policy Committee was due to review on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday the sale process, seen as a key gauge of investor\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sentiment and the government's commitment to reform.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par PESO STRUGGLES AT 50\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso poked above the 50-per-dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par level, as covering of short dollar positions triggered on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday abated.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     But dealers in Manila said the rally soon petered out with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the peso ending at 50.07, from Monday's onshore close of 50.53.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Earlier, the peso touched a session high of 49.95, far from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday's 13-month high of 49.20.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Dealers said the peso was supported by equity-related\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflows and remittances by overseas workers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Data released at the weekend showed the balance of payments\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recorded a $654 million surplus for January-February, reversing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a year-ago deficit of $1.087 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Tuesday's data showed the March volume of production index\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par fell 1.2 percent from same period year ago, while the value of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par production index shrank 2.9 percent. Average capacity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par utilisation was at 75.6 percent, from April's 75.4 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said no physical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par was detected even after Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par peso could rise to 48 per dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S.       $0929 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     124.49       124.86         +0.30\par  Sing dlr      1.7929       1.7983         +0.30\par  Taiwan dlr    34.309       34.375         +0.19\par  Korean won   1218.80      1233.80         +1.23\par  Baht           42.57        42.82         +0.59\par  Peso           50.06        50.53         +0.94\par  Rupiah       8850.00      8890.00         +0.45\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.8000         +0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     124.49       131.24         +5.42\par  Sing dlr      1.7929       1.8515         +3.27\par  Taiwan dlr    34.309       34.986         +1.97\par  Korean won   1218.80      1277.00         +4.78\par  Baht          