\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won Hits 17-Month High \b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2002\par 12:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar continued their assault against the U.S. dollar Monday, advancing to fresh 17-month and 11-month respective highs. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah also gained on the dollar, while the Philippine peso slipped. Trading in the Singapore dollar and Thai baht was limited because markets in Singapore and Bangkok were closed for public holidays. \par \par \uc2  The yen wasn't much of a factor in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it was trapped in a narrow range against the dollar due to the Memorial Day holiday in the U.S. Monday. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korean officials had only a minimal impact on the won as it strengthened to its best finish against the dollar since December 2000. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,233.8 won, down from KRW1,243.2 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Jeon Yun-churl said that while the appreciation of the won basically reflects South Korea's economic fundamentals, the government is concerned over the speed of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The won has appreciated 8% against the dollar since mid-April. \par \par \uc2  The won dropped back after Jeon's comments, but the retreat was brief because few market players believe the government will actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Our government has no intention to buy U.S. dollars in the market," said Gin Lee, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales trading at BNP Paribas in Seoul. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be very difficult because of worries about inflation." \par \par \uc2  Allowing the won to strengthen tends to reduce the cost of imported goods and keeps inflationary pressures from building. \par \par \uc2  Dollar selling was widespread, with exporters dumping the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the most aggressively, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar edged higher in thin trading, closing at its highest level against the U.S. dollar since June 2001. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at NT$34.375 compared with NT$34.414 Friday. \par \par \uc2  With Singapore markets closed during Asian hours and with the U.S. holiday, turnover was thin, one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Many participants were also sidelined because of fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dealer added. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a regular basis to stave off market volatility, was relatively inactive. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso fell to a four-week low against the dollar amid dollar short-covering by banks and rising corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.530 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP50.350 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said some banks opted to cover their short-dollar positions on growing perceptions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't move to support the peso. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah strengthened despite market talk that efforts to sell PT Bank Niaga may have hit rocky ground. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,880 rupiah, its intraday low, versus IDR8,950 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian officials remained quiet over the fate of PT Bank Niaga, a small bank that has become the latest test of the country's resolve to fix its broken banking system. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jakarta set Monday as the deadline for final bids for Niaga from four\par shortlisted investors. Market speculation swirled that Indonesia is\par likely to reject the bids as being too low and reopen the bidding.\par   -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020527dy5r000lp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
