\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Dn Late;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Concerns Grip Market \b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 10:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was weaker Friday as concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed on sentiment after another wave of U.S. dollar buying by the Bank of Japan late Thursday sent the yen sliding, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0825 GMT (4:25 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7990, up from S$1.7965 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.7980 and S$1.8010 intraday in thin trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened around S$1.8005 in Asia but dropped sharply as U.S. banks took advantage of the overnight U.S. dollar rise to take profits, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But trade thinned after the U.S. dollar fell below S$1.8000 support, triggering fears that such a drop might force the hand of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were also less inclined to try and initiate fresh positions, especially ahead of a long weekend in Singapore and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's financial markets will be closed for a religious holiday Monday, while markets in the U.S. will be closed for the Memorial Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a lot quieter today. Earlier on, many people, mostly U.S. banks, were just taking profit, but now that's eased," a Japanese bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the volatile movements of the U.S. dollar against its regional counterparts during the week had hurt market participants, especially with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, so many were just staying on the sidelines, waiting for the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the U.S. dollar is expected to continue to be thinly traded against the local dollar as market participants adjust positions. Support for the U.S. dollar is expected at S$1.7950 and resistance at S$1.8030, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended mixed late Friday in light trading, with many market participants staying on the sidelines ahead of the long weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds fell marginally in early trading, tracking modest falls in U.S. Treasurys overnight, but most tenors managed to recoup part of their losses on bargain-hunting, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down two basis points at 1.60%, while the yield on five-year bonds remained unchanged at 2.96%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was steady at 3.57% while the yield on 10-year bonds inched up one basis point to 3.94%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was steady at 4.41%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer was unchanged at 3.73% while the 10-year swap offer held steady at\par 4.07%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened slightly to 215\par basis points from 214 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were also mixed, though liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.6250% 0.7500%   0.6875% 0.8125%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.1875% 1.3125%   1.1250% 1.2500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020524dy5o000i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
