\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 12:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed in narrow trading ranges Thursday as market participants remained cautious of further U.S. dollar buying by Japanese authorities to curb the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the second straight day, following through on the MOF's threat to "take action when necessary" to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair in line. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities pulled the trigger when the U.S. dollar fell through the Y124 level after the Asian session had closed and movements were largely being driven by trading in London. The U.S. dollar shot up to Y124.90 within minutes after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had traded in a tight Y124-124.15 range during Asian hours as fears of possible MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hung over the market. Most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were similarly rangebound. \par \par \uc2  "The market will continue to be wary of the BOJ, with expectations that it may have to act again in the current dollar-heavy environment," said Emmanuel Ng, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  After a long period of strength, the U.S. dollar has languished over the past few months as flat corporate profits in the U.S. have sent investors looking for better opportunities in Asia and elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, New Taiwan dollar and Indonesian rupiah strengthened. The Singapore dollar and Philippine peso finished marginally weaker, while the Thai baht was unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The won hit another 15-month high on investor optimism over South Korea's economic recovery. The country is pegged to be Asia's top performer this year as structural reforms have boosted economic efficiency and turned a current account deficit into a surplus. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at 1,246.1 won, down from Wednesday's finish at KRW1,247.2. The U.S. dollar traded between KRW1,245.8 and KRW1,250.0. \par \par \uc2  Although foreigners were net sellers of Korean stocks in the first quarter, strong earnings results by Korean companies should lure those investors back, Barclays Capital said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  Continued inflows of foreign direct investment and Korea's relatively attractive interest rates are also supporting the Korean won, Barclays said. \par \par \uc2  "In the long run, because a significant share of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows to Korea are structural, it will be difficult to resist an appreciation of the won," the report said. \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020523dy5n000q7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
