\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down Late As Yen Falls On MOF  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 11:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was weaker late Wednesday, in tandem with the yen's fall after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  At 0930 GMT (5:30 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7940, up from S$1.7930 late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.7865 and S$1.7970. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a low of S$1.7865 in early trading but soon recovered as market players became concerned that such a sudden sharp move lower may provoke the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's wrath. \par \par \uc2  "The cautiousness is still there, but the overall (U.S.) dollar weakness is the biggest factor now," said a European bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  There has been some speculation in the market recently that the MAS may actually have stepped in to buy U.S. dollars, albeit quietly and in modest amounts, to iron out excess volatility in the Singapore dollar's moves. \par \par \uc2  After a MAS official said Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was comfortable with the Singapore dollar's current levels - a statement some traders took to mean the MAS would allow the local dollar to strengthen further without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the U.S. dollar fell through crucial support at S$1.8000. It later broke below S$1.7900 Monday. \par \par \uc2  But the sharp U.S. dollar rebound around mid-afternoon Wednesday came about not because of the MAS, but was due to the Japanese finance ministry's efforts to stem the U.S. dollar's slide against the yen, dealers in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Market participants in the local market scrambled to cover their short positions in the U.S. dollar built up since last week, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to be supported at S$1.7920 and resisted at S$1.7980. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mixed Wednesday as profit-taking robbed some tenors of part of their early gains, after the overnight rise in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down one basis point at 1.60%, while the yield on five-year bonds was flat at 2.98%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was also steady at 3.59% while the yield on 10-year bonds rose one basis point to 3.96%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was one basis point lower at 4.41%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer fell three basis points to 3.75% while the 10-year swap offer\par slipped two basis points to 4.10%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 218 basis points\par from 214 basis points late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity was\par sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Wednesday    Late Tuesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.6875% 0.8125%   0.7500% 0.8125%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.8125% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.1250% 1.2500%   1.1250% 1.2500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020522dy5m000k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
