\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down As Yen Pulls Back \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ryan Woo \par \uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2002\par 13:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed off from recent rises, taking the tone from a falling yen Tuesday and as stock markets around the region tumbled. \par \par \uc2  Lingering concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by some regional central banks added to the pressure on local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting investors to cover their U.S. dollar short positions. \par \par \uc2  However, near-term sentiment toward the dollar remains bleak, and traders expect Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume their upward move after a short period of consolidation. \par \par \uc2  Only the Indonesian rupiah ended the day higher as investors bet the government will forge ahead with the sale of shares in several state-owned companies and assets controlled by the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency. \par \par \uc2  Comments from the International Monetary Fund - that Indonesia's macroeconomic situation has improved in recent weeks, reflected in the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining inflation, and lower interest rates - also aided the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The IMF added it made "good progress" during initial discussions with Indonesia aimed at reaching a new loan agreement under the fund's $5 billion program for the country. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed lower at IDR9,030, compared with IDR9,050 Monday. \par \par \uc2  For most of the other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, the yen's fortunes held sway. \par \par \uc2  A flurry of comments by Japanese policymakers voicing increasing discomfort over the yen's recent surge knocked the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar off their multi-month highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0903 GMT (5:03 a.m. EDT), the dollar was at Y125.65, above Y125.36 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y125.78 late Monday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Rumors the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore was in the market aided the Singapore dollar's decline, while lingering concerns over similar action by the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also pressured the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  South Korea, Hong Kong, Taiwan Shares Down \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Park Seung made it clear the won's recent rally against the U.S. dollar wasn't welcome. He didn't elaborate but there are concerns the won's persistent strength could erode export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, shares of Korean exporters fell Tuesday on those concerns as well as on weaker DRAM prices - Korea's exports are driven by DRAM products, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Today there was good demand for dollars (against the won), but I don't think it's permanent," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment over the won, however, remains positive as investors continue to expect the Korean economy to grow faster than earlier anticipated. \par \par \uc2  The government is due to announce first quarter gross domestic product data Wednesday. A survey of 10 economists by Dow Jones Newswires expect the economy to grow 4.9% on year in the first quarter or a seasonally adjusted 1.2% on quarter. The government predicts a slightly slower 4.7% year-on-year expansion, and follows a 3.7% rise in the fourth quarter of last year. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar finished higher against the won, at KRW1,254.6, following four sessions of decline. The dollar ended at KRW1,253.6 Monday. \par \par \uc2  It isn't just the won which has surged on optimism over the economy. The Singapore dollar, which has benefited from the island nation's increasingly bullish economic outlook, returned some of its recent gains Tuesday on the yen's retreat. Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7930, up from S$1.7910 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday to slow the local dollar's rise did the rounds again. Some dealers suspected the MAS bought the U.S. dollar yesterday after it fell through support at S$1.7920. \par \par \uc2  Such market talk isn't surprising as the issue of the country's export competitiveness remain on the government's agenda and joblessness continue to tick higher. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the New Taiwan dollar also ended lower as traders covered their short positions in the U.S. dollar. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished at NT$34.415, up from NT$34.412 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan shares fell for the fourth consecutive session Tuesday, hit by a selloff on Wall Street. But dealers said the focus of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains on the yen, whose uptrend is expected to stay in place. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso finished lower largely on the yen, and also after a power outage hit the capital and the rest of the main island of Luzon. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar moved up as some traders speculated that something bad may happen," said a trader, referring to the flurry of coup rumors that often accompany power outages in the Philippines - a throwback from a number of coup attempts during the presidency of Corazon Aquino in the late 1980s. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to PHP49.465 from PHP49.355 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar stood at THB42.66 late in the day, compared with THB42.585 late Monday. During the day, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakulasaid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ruled out because "we (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) won't do anything as the baht is moving in line with the (region's) market (trend)." \par \par \uc2  The baht hit a 15-month high early Monday at THB42.53 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Ryan Woo, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4136; ryan.woo@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020521dy5l000r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
