\par\par \b \uc2  US Dollar Below S$1.8000 Vs Singapore Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2002\par 03:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The U.S. dollar lurched below the crucial S$1.8000 level against the Singapore dollar early Friday, as positive policy pronouncements from the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped weaken the U.S. dollar against the local unit, traders said Friday. \par \par \uc2  The strength in the Singapore dollar came amid a broader regional weakness of the U.S. dollar Friday, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, or MAS, Friday said it was comfortable with current levels for the Singapore dollar, after a quarterly economic survey published earlier Friday confirmed market beliefs that the economy was on the path of recovery. \par \par \uc2  MAS' Assistant Managing Director Khor Hoe Ee told reporters that the trade weighted Singapore dollar is still within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undisclosed policy band, "and we are comfortable with it." \par \par \uc2  Some market participants took the MAS statement to mean they might tolerate a stronger local dollar, and coming at a time when the U.S. dollar is retaining a weak tone across the region, the fall was inevitable, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market expected the MAS to defend the (U.S.) dollar at S$1.8000, but after the statement, the market took it as a cue to take it lower," an Asian bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Khor also said that the Singapore dollar's recent appreciation against the U.S. dollar is due to the fact that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has broadly declined against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar plunged through its recent seven-month low of S$1.7988, a level not seen since mid-October last year, soon after the MAS statement. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, an aggressive bout of U.S. dollar buying pulled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off its low and sparked fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But in the past few weeks, with the U.S. dollar giving up more ground against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants have become emboldened to test the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the local dollar, but have stayed shy of the S$1.8000 mark. \par \par \uc2  Around 0205 GMT (10.05 p.m. EDT) Friday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7968, compared with its early opening level of S$1.8052. \par \par \uc2  -By Sai Man and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020517dy5h0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
