\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Dlr Can't Shake Poor Sentiment \b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2002\par 12:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to grind higher against the dollar Monday in a relatively lackluster trading session. \par \par \uc2  With a dearth of market-moving news to drive trading, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shadowed the yen and euro, both of which edged higher amid ongoing investor worries over the U.S. economic recovery and prices of equities and corporate bonds. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won hit a five-month high against the dollar, although one dealer said "there was little momentum" in the market. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, the Singapore dollar, Thai baht, New Taiwan dollar and Philippine peso all gained on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks continued to languish Friday, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average closing down 1% at 9939.32 and the Nasdaq Composite Index finishing 3% lower at 1600.87. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest slump is stirring a debate in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as to whether the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long bull run may be over. \par \par \uc2  "On a trade weighted basis, the U.S. dollar has very probably peaked," Bank of America said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  The bank noted that until now the dollar has been able to shrug off the potential ill effects of the rising U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  However, the worries "now carry greater conviction amidst nerves about the health of corporate America," according to the report. \par \par \uc2  One thing the dollar has working in its favor, analysts say, is that neither investments in the euro-zone economies, nor in Japan are looking particularly attractive these days. \par \par \uc2  "Wanting to sell the greenback is one thing, finding an alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth buying is something very different," Bank of America said. \par \par \uc2  The rising yen pulled the won higher, despite continued foreign outflows from the Korean stock market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at KRW1,277.4 won, down from Friday's close of KRW1,280.0. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners sold a net KRW123.8 billion in shares Monday, bringing total outflows to more than KRW900 billion so far this month. \par \par \uc2  But that was more than offset by won buying in sympathy with the yen's gains. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was quoted at Y127.28 in late Asian trade, down from Y127.55 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was also underpinned by strength in the yen, although losses in the local stock market capped its gains. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.550, down from Friday's NT$34.573. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar traded between NT$34.52-NT$34.55 for most of the session, although it did briefly hit a high of NT$34.63 in the opening minutes of trading. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar strengthened after trading in a tight range against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8035, down from S$1.8070 late Friday. It traded between S$1.8025 and S$1.8040. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's strength was limited by concerns that excessive moves would draw \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which guides the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was buoyed by stronger regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued remittance inflows. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP49.505 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP49.620 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said many players took their cue from the U.S. unit's continued slide against the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against the rupiah, the dollar was quoted at IDR9,275 near the end of Asian trading, down from IDR9,290 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreign banks were among the dollar sellers Monday reflecting that they're still optimistic the rupiah will rise further as the government's pending 51% stake sale of PT Bank Niaga is expected to be successful. \par \par \uc2  The baht mirrored other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin trading volume. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 42.840 baht, down from THB42.900 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020513dy5d000te\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
