\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; Dollar Rebounds Across Region \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2002\par 12:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- A rebound against the yen helped the dollar break its string of losses against several regional Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished Asian trading hours above 128 yen for the first time in six sessions, inspired by a strong report on first-quarter U.S. labor productivity released overnight and a bullish earnings report from computer-networking equipment maker Cisco Systems Inc. after the close of markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  A possible Moody's Investor Service downgrade of Japan's sovereign rating, and a new round of jawboning from \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials also kept the yen under pressure. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, repeated the MOF's now familiar refrain suggesting that the ministry may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's strength if it appreciates too far against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading the dollar was quoted at Y128.55, up from Y127.70 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's strength flowed through to other \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the region, beating back a number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had hit multi-month highs against the dollar recently. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar gained on the New Taiwan dollar for the first time in 17 sessions as the yen's fall encouraged short-covering in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.588, up from NT$34.565 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also garnered some support as the result of a slowdown in the closing of U.S. dollar deposit accounts by Taiwanese investors. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's apparent goal of keeping the U.S. dollar above NT$34.50 gave depositors incentive to hold onto the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won fell against the dollar for the first time in five sessions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,285.4 won, up from Tuesday's close at a four-month low of KRW1,279.5. \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground early in the session as exporters sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of their normal trading activities, but recovered later on short covering on the back of the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  However, the won is likely to outperform the dollar and yen in the months ahead as a result of South Korea's strengthening economy, according to Bank of America. The bank is forecasting the dollar to fall to KRW1,180 and the yen to KRW9.5 by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso's fall broke a string of 10 straight sessions of gains against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP49.820 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP49.640 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "This is a healthy correction for the peso," said a trader with a local bank, noting that the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to resume its advance against the dollar in the near term because remittance and portfolio investment inflows remain strong. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its overnight rates unchanged at its board meeting Wednesday, in line with the current neutral stance of the U.S. Federal Reserve. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's headline overnight borrowing rate is 7%, the lowest in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell in line with the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8092, up from S$1.8023 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also benefited from worries about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah closed slightly lower in quiet trading as players closed their short dollar positions ahead of a public holiday Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 9,300 rupiah, up from IDR9,280 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Underlying sentiment remains bullish on the rupiah following the recent sale of a 51% stake in PT Bank Central Asia, the Paris Club's $5.4 billion debt restructuring, and a $340 million loan disbursement from the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar was quoted at THB42.945 late in Asia, up from THB42.875 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020508dy58000tq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
