\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Wall Street weighs on Asia stocks, dollar glum. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 7 (Reuters) - Wall Street's losses cast a pall over Asian stocks on Tuesday, with Tokyo worst off after a four-day break, while the dollar hit fresh multi-month lows against the yen, euro and Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold extended Asian gains to open at 26-month highs of $312.40/312.90 a troy ounce in Europe as London traders returned from Monday's holiday to a weaker dollar, reinforcing the metal's safe haven appeal. \par \par \uc2  Escalating \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warnings from Japanese officials brought only a temporary reprieve to the dollar, which reached two-month lows on the yen and seven-month troughs on the euro and Swiss franc . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 126.85/91 yen at 0650 GMT. \par \par \uc2  A near universal expectation that the Federal Reserve would stand pat on interest rates made Tuesday's policy meeting a non-factor, with softer U.S. data in recent weeks suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not tighten till the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  Asian dollar bonds rallied as investors rushed back to buy higher yielding regional credits, which offered handsome bargains after the recent weakness in spreads. \par \par \uc2  But South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets with a quarter point rate hike, its first rise since October 2000, sending stocks lower and major sovereign yields sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea said its move was aimed at curbing inflation pressures but it came earlier in the year than economists expected and counter to government claims on Sunday that no rise was imminent. \par \par \uc2  TOKYO TRIPS \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei share average fell 2.03 percent or 234.97 points to 11,316.04, led by steep falls in high-tech shares as traders returned from the Golden Week break to face a 2.14 percent fall in the U.S. Nasdaq , its 12th down day in the last 14 trading sessions. \par \par \uc2  "A U.S. economic recovery is an important prerequisite for a "V"-shaped earnings recovery here. However, as last week's jobs data suggested, the U.S. recovery may not be as strong as we had hoped," said Masayoshi Yano, an investment information manager at Tokai Tokyo Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Also, another important pillar in that recovery scenario is a softer yen, but it is actually firming." \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's immediate upside was blocked by option-related dollar bids around 126.50/60 yen and talk a Japanese public fund may be supporting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of a downgrade when Moody's Investors Service completes its review of Japan's credit rating next week also gave yen bulls some pause. \par \par \uc2  Although the Japanese Finance Ministry's unease with the yen's rise was largely ignored, traders avoided pushing the dollar below 1.5800 Swiss francs fearing protests by the Swiss National Bank. \par \par \uc2  Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also made limited headway in the face of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar backing off early seven-month highs amid rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have bought dollars to slow its rise. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS SAG \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Seoul shares posted their third consecutive decline, with the Korea Composite Stock Price Index falling 0.06 percent to 826.34 as investors gave a thumbs-down to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pre-emptive rate hike. \par \par \uc2  "There has been consensus that interest rates will be raised but I think the timing was not appropriate given that uncertainties are growing about economic growth," said Kim Jin-sung, an analyst at Kyobo Securities in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was down 0.21 percent or 24.18 points at 11,712.90 at 0645 GMT, off its early lows on hopes for a sustainable domestic recovery in the second half. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, the Straits Times Index was just 0.05 percent or 0.86 point at 1,721.19 percent at 0646 GMT, while Taiwan's weighted TAIEX stood out with a 0.38 percent rise to finish at 5,663.98 as bargain-hunters emerged after Monday's 4.5 percent slump to a two-month low. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020507dy5700dqn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
