\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Gains Muted By Yen Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 12:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar weakened against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, but a late session rally against the yen helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trim its losses in the region. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped nearly one full yen in the last hour of Asian trading, providing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with at least a temporary respite from its recent downward slide against Japanese unit. \par \par \uc2  Dealers dismissed any speculation that the move may have been engineered by the Bank of Japan, saying it was more likely a result of the dollar having been oversold against the yen in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an intraday low of Y126.75 early in the Asian day without attracting much attention. But a bounce back above this level late in the session triggered a round of dollar buying that caught many market players off guard. The dollar quickly shot up to Y127.60 as players were forced to buy back the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cover their short positions. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at Y127.70, up from Y127.16 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but closed well off its intraday lows. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Thai baht and Philippine peso all strengthened. The New Taiwan dollar and Indonesian rupiah were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Investor sentiment continues to run against the dollar globally due to concerns about the strength of the economic recovery in the U.S. and questions over the outlook for U.S. corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers in Asia don't appear overly concerned about the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, said Jimmy Koh, head of Treasury Research at United Overseas Bank. \par \par \uc2  While the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are gaining on the dollar, on a trade-weighted basis most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain fairly competitive against each other, he said. \par \par \uc2  "You may see some central banks trying to slow down the rate of appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they're not in the market trying to put a cap on them," Koh said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso advanced to a 13-month high against the dollar amid weak demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP49.640 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP49.775 Monday, but off its intraday low of PHP49.440. It was the dollar's weakest finish since March 2001. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor appeared to give his blessing to the move, acknowledging in a radio interview that analysts were predicting the dollar could fall as low as PHP48-PHP49 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar briefly touched a seven-month high against the U.S. dollar before falling back on concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen's late slide. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8023, down from S$1.8047 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell through the key psychological S$1.8000 mark against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the session on its way to an intraday low of S$1.7988, its weakest performance since mid-October last year. \par \par \uc2  The won strengthened for the fourth straight session to yet another four-month high against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,279.5 won, down from Monday's close at KRW1,281.2. The dollar traded as low as KRW1,276.6 during the session. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Frank Gong said the dollar is likely due for some consolidation given its recent losses against the won. The won has gained 4% against the dollar since closing at a one-year low of KRW1,332.0 in mid-April. \par \par \uc2  The baht extended its recent gains against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at 42.875 baht, down from THB42.950 late Monday in offshore trade. The baht wasn't traded onshore Monday because markets in Bangkok were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a 14-month intraday low of THB42.81 in afternoon trading before recovering slightly in line with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's late rebound against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was unchanged against the U.S. dollar, snapping a string of 15 straight gains. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.656, unchanged from Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was weaker for most of the session, but gained late in the session on the back of the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was unchanged as dollar bids from local companies offset dollar selling by foreign banks still bullish on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed unchanged at 9,280 rupiah after trading in a tight range of between IDR9,275 and IDR9,290. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020507dy57000ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
