\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, But Off 7-Mo High On MAS Concerns \b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 10:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar strengthened Tuesday, briefly touching a seven-month high against the U.S. dollar, before slipping back on concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0825 GMT (4:25 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8023, down from S$1.8047 late Monday. It traded between S$1.7988 and S$1.8028 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened around S$1.8005 against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, hurt by persistent selling on U.S. dollar rallies in favor of its stronger regional counterparts, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar subsequently slumped below the key psychological S$1.8000 mark against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and proceeded to its intraday low of S$1.7988, its weakest since mid-October last year. \par \par \uc2  However, sellers became spooked when local banks bought U.S. dollars heavily, bringing it back up above S$1.8000, and sparking fears they may be acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary-policy tool in seeking to maintain brisk growth and low inflation. The Singapore dollar is guided within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  Although the possible MAS action couldn't be confirmed, market participants took on a more cautious note, and the U.S. dollar soon recouped part of its overnight losses, as local banks continued their purchases. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the U.S. dollar is expected to trade in a tight range against the Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy-setting body, the Federal Open Market Committee, later Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Support for the U.S. dollar is expected at S$1.7950, with resistance likely at S$1.8050. \par \par \uc2  Singapore government bond prices were mostly higher late Tuesday, as domestic interbank rates stayed weak, keeping the costs of borrowing low, and as investors remained confident the FOMC will keep U.S. interest rates steady, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds opened higher, defying the overnight drop in U.S. Treasurys, though trading volumes were still relatively small, with some market participants preferring to wait on the sidelines until after the FOMC announces its decision late Tuesday, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down two basis points at 1.54%, while the yield on five-year bonds slipped one basis point to 2.91%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was two basis points lower at 3.53% while the yield on 10-year bonds was down one basis point at 3.87%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was flat at 4.37%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps were lower with the\par seven-year swap offer down two basis points at 3.69% while the 10-year\par swap offer slipped one basis point to 4.05%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 219 basis points\par from 216 basis points late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.5625% 0.6875%   0.4375% 0.5625%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.1250% 1.2500%   1.1250% 1.2500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020507dy57000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
