\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; US Dlr Under Siege \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 941 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2002\par 12:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended their recent uptrend late Thursday, as the U.S. dollar remained under siege and many of the region's stock markets regained their footing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar suffered another blow after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill's remarks overnight heightened speculation that Washington was backing away from its commitment to a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  After a sharp yen-and-half plunge overnight, the dollar fell for most of the Asian session as traders pondered the implications of O'Neill's failure to state clearly that the U.S. stands by a strong dollar, in his high-profile Congressional testimony Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  After briefly dipping below Y127 earlier in the day, the dollar clambered to Y127.26 at 0950 GMT, but was still below Y127.44 late Wednesday in New York and Y128.56 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The near-term direction of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depend on whether the U.S. dollar would crack key levels against its other major counterparts, namely the euro's resistance at $0.9100 and the U.S. dollar's support of Y126.50 against the yen, said Thio Chin Loo, a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  As most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained buoyed at multi-month highs, speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  made their rounds in the Thai, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But it was only in Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market where dealers felt unequivocal about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presence. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso surged to its strongest level in almost a year, boosted in part by relief that antigovernment protests passed off peacefully Wednesday and the prospect of U.S. pension fund Calpers reversing a decision to pull investments out of the country, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  California Public Employees' Retirement System, or Calpers, pulled out of four Asian emerging markets in February due to stricter investment guidelines, but Manila lobbied successfully for reinclusion. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's denial that it had bought dollars to damp the peso's rally also emboldened participants to push the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, dealers said. There was talk in the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought around $25 million at PHP50.50 to boost reserves earlier Thursday, but died down when the U.S. dollar continued its downward spiral, dealers in Manila said. \par \par \uc2  In fact, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Amando Tetangco said it was possible for the dollar to extend its slide to PHP50 soon, although he didn't provide a timeframe. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.20 on the Philippine Dealing System - its lowest close since May 17, 2001 - down 0.7% from PHP50.54 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Worries that antigovernment demonstrations by supporters of ousted leader Joseph Estrada and leftist groups May 1 would be marred by violence kept support for the dollar well buttressed at PHP50.50 in recent trading days. \par \par \uc2  Despite fears that the region's central banks might curb the appreciation of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said they are likely to tolerate some strength - provided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are stable on a trade-weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  As the Seoul stock market climbed 2.1%, the South Korean won closed at a fresh four-month high of KRW1,288.0 against the dollar, compared with KRW1,294.0 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar muscled through NT$34.700 resistance to close at a four-and-a-half month peak against its U.S. counterpart as it found additional support from U.S. dollar sales by exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar purchases by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - up to an estimated US$400 million - and a 3.3% plunge on the Taipei bourse failed to salvage the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.645, lower than NT$34.717 Tuesday. Dealings totaled US$698 million. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to 43.150 baht to the dollar, compared with THB43.225 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  There was market talk that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars via banks in Thailand when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at the day's low of around THB43.100. \par \par \uc2  "But we didn't really see anything," said a dealer at an Asian bank in Singapore, adding that there was little justification for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the baht was merely adjusting to the recent gains in the yen and the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The baht should be at a comfortable level at the moment," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's business sentiment improved significantly in March, rising above the 50-point level for the first time in 14 months, indicating that the businessmen who were surveyed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expect future business conditions to improve. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Business Sentiment Index - based on a survey of businessmen at medium-to-large firms - was at 53.5 points in March, up from 49.9 in February and 49.2 in January, it said in a monthly report posted on its Web site. \par \par \uc2  A reading below 50 points means that most respondents expect future business conditions to worsen. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted at S$1.8094 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , marginally stronger compared with S$1.8097 late Tuesday. The benchmark stock index closed 0.9% higher. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets in the Philippines, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and South Korea were closed Wednesday for Labor Day. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady at 9,340 rupiah to the dollar, compared with IDR9,335 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020502dy52000t9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
