\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Stock Selloff Curbs Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2002\par 11:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit fresh multi-month highs late Monday as the U.S. dollar remained on the defensive - particularly against the yen - in international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  However, the selloff in regional equity markets - weighed by losses on Wall Street - and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept a rein on the strength of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , preventing them from breaking key resistance levels, observers said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen would appreciate and outperform Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the central banks want to maintain some measure of competitiveness," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the dollar slipped to as low as Y127.65, having shed three yen in just a week. Trading was thin Monday with Tokyo markets closed. \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT (5:00 a.m. EDT), the dollar had reclaimed some ground and was quoted at Y127.83, compared with Y127.80 in New York, and Y128.79 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains helped push the Singapore dollar to six-and-a-half month high, the South Korean won to a four-month peak, the New Taiwan dollar to a fresh four-month high and the Philippine peso to seven-month high. The Thai baht rode on the coattails of its regional cousins. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants were increasingly nervous about the prospects of a collapse in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although some analysts ruled out such a possibility. A further slide in the dollar against the yen could extend the recent rally in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar index, trading around 114.93, was approaching key long-term trendlines, a break of which could end the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long period of strength. \par \par \uc2  "It is actually a very fine call now," said Mohi-uddin at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is at a very critical juncture at the moment, but I don't think the dollar will collapse just yet," he said. "The corporate bond market is still attracting inflows, and European fundamentals are lagging those of the U.S." \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at S$1.8020, its weakest level since Oct. 10 last year, after having been sold off in New York trade Friday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar purchases by Singapore and U.S. banks helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regain its footing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rebound also led some in the market to surmise that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, although some observers dismissed such speculation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Though we think the Singapore dollar is at the stronger end of the (policy) band, we are skeptical that it is strong enough to attract the attention of the authorities," Bank of America said in a report. The MAS guides the Singapore dollar within a trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and key competitors. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the Singapore dollar was stronger at S$1.8067, compared with S$1.8097 against its U.S. counterpart late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Month-end dollar sales by exporters provided an additional fillip for the South Korean won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won finished at KRW1,293.6 against the dollar, stronger than Friday's close of KRW1,297.6. The won hit an intraday high of KRW1,289.0. \par \par \uc2  Short-covering in U.S. dollar positions emerged late in the day after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to sustain its break below KRW1,290, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Net sales of some KRW208.6 billion worth of South Korean shares by foreign investors somewhat restrained the won's rise, although it failed to reverse its trend. \par \par \uc2  Helped in part by exporters' dollar-sales, the New Taiwan dollar finished at NT$34.730 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stronger compared with NT$34.769 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Intermittent U.S.-dollar purchases by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreigner equity sales of a net NT$5.22 billion worth of shares on the Taiwan Stock Exchange Monday helped put a lid on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the peso closed at PHP50.630 to the dollar, compared with PHP50.823 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar managed to break below its long-standing support at PHP50.80 this morning on expectations of more inflows from overseas remittances, as well as positive sentiment following reports of U.S. pension fund Calpers is likely to review its decision to pull out of the Manila stock market," Bank of America said in a report. \par \par \uc2  Philippine Finance Secretary Jose Isidro Camacho said Friday that U.S. pension fund Calpers was prepared to review its decision to divest its equity holdings in the country. The California Public Employees' Retirement System, or Calpers, decided in February to pull out of four Asian emerging markets due to stricter investment guidelines. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was firmer at THB43.205 to the dollar, compared with THB43.260 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Left out in the cold, the Indonesian rupiah retreated after having priced in news that the International Monetary Fund had agreed Friday to unlock an additional $347 million in loans to the country. \par \par \uc2  The fund said the Indonesian government has broadly fulfilled the conditions of a $4.6 billion IMF loan aimed at rebuilding the economy. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at IDR9,325 to the dollar, compared with IDR9,300 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020429dy4t000md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
