\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Singapore Dollar Soars \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2002\par 12:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to rally Friday as global sentiment toward the dollar was damped by concerns over the U.S. economic recovery and disappointing corporate earnings. \par \par \uc2  The yen set the tone for the region, trading below Y129 per dollar for the second day in a row. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was among the stronger regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, breaking through key resistance levels on its way to a six-month high. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar powered its way through the psychologically important threshold of S$1.8100 per U.S. dollar on its way to an intraday low of S$1.8070. It was the U.S. dollar's poorest showing since last October. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar recovered somewhat to finish Asian trading at S$1.8097, down from S$1.8120 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar, Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah also gained on the dollar. The South Korean won was unchanged and the Thai baht edged lower. \par \par \uc2  Most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained near multi-month highs against the dollar reached earlier in the week following a round of weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  The disappointing U.S. data, along with continuing negative corporate news and concerns about the sustainability of the enormous U.S. current account deficit, are likely to keep pressure on the dollar in the near term, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "This shift in sentiment is set to continue for some time, making us cautious on the dollar," UBS Warburg \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Mansoor Mohi-uddin said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar weakness isn't the "start of a collapse" and the UBS Warburg's report predicts the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue its upward trend over the longer term. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. current account deficit, which covers trade in goods and services as well as investment earnings, has become a major concern for the dollar. The U.S. must attract an estimated $1 billion a day from abroad to finance the shortfall, which stands at about 4% of gross domestic product. It is those capital inflows that have helped underpin the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The fear is that if foreigners lose interest in U.S. assets, and the inflows dry up, the dollar will undergo a rapid and sizable downward correction. \par \par \uc2  A recent study by the Federal Reserve of 25 episodes of large adjustments in the current account deficit of developed countries showed that the shortfalls usually began to reverse once they exceeded 5% of GDP. The adjustments were typically accompanied by a 10% to 25% fall in the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  An increasingly rosy outlook for the Singapore economy this year compared with more modest expectations for the U.S. are working in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's favor, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  However, it will be difficult for the Singapore dollar to appreciate significantly going forward, analyst say. Recent moves in the Singapore dollar have probably resulted in its shift to the stronger half of a trading band used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Any further movement toward the band's limit could prompt the MAS to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's strength, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  There was some speculation in the market Friday that the MAS may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but many analysts dismissed this. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar closed at a four-month high as the yen's rise inspired U.S. dollar selling by Taiwanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.769, compared with NT$34.783 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore and local banks' short-covering of U.S. dollar positions and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined to lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its intraday low of NT$34.741. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, a mix of offsetting factors left the won unchanged from its four-month high a day earlier. The dollar closed at KRW1,297.6. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners' net sales of KRW279 billion in local shares convinced some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  players to build long-dollar positions. But the rising yen prompted others to shed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In addition, exporters sold dollars as part of their regular month-end book balancing. \par \par \uc2  With no fresh leads to drive peso trading, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked the yen and other regionals higher. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.823 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP50.835 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah strengthened modestly as sentiment continues to improve toward Indonesia's debt restructuring efforts. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at IDR9,300, down from IDR9,360 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investor Services raised Indonesia's sovereign outlook to positive from stable this week due to the country's successful $5.4 billion debt rescheduling with the Paris Club of creditors earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Corporate demand and dollar short-covering kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firm against the baht. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at THB43.260, up from THB43.210 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020426dy4q000wv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
