\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Hits 6-Month High Against US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2002\par 11:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar rose to a six-month high against the U.S. dollar Thursday, as local and foreign banks sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with its broader decline against Asian units, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0907 GMT (5:07 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8126, down from S$1.8190 late Wednesday, but off its intraday low of S$1.8108 - its lowest level since mid-October. Trading volume was moderate to heavy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.8160 in Asia and fell through the day on selling led by local banks. Market participants unwound long U.S.-dollar positions in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline against the yen and other Asian units. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell below Y129 to an intraday low of Y128.77. \par \par \uc2  In late afternoon trading, U.S.-dollar selling by U.S. banks and investment houses pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below an important 200-day moving average support at S$1.8121 to hit an intraday low of S$1.8108. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar quickly bounced off its low as participants feared the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been oversold and cautiously bought it back to ward off possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from sliding too sharply. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysis shows the U.S. dollar's intraday fall to below the key support at a 200-day moving average is very bearish, as it seems to signal an end to the medium-term sideways consolidation that has prevailed since November. \par \par \uc2  This is the first time since October that the U.S. dollar has fallen below its 200-day moving average against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the U.S. dollar is now appears to be initially targeting a decline to S$1.8070, but a drop below that level is quite possible if it continues to slide against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's upside is expected to be capped at S$1.8170, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore government bonds extended their recent rally Thursday, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight and on expectations inflation and interest rates in Singapore will remain low this year as the economy recovers gradually and the Federal Reserve keeps U.S. rates on hold at least until August. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said shorter-dated bonds were also boosted by an expected supply shortfall as S$3.28 billion of one- and five-year paper maturing May 14 and May 15 will be replaced only by S$2.20 billion worth of one-year Treasury bills to be issued next week. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds fell three basis points to 3.60%, while the five-year bond yield ended six basis points lower at 3.01%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven-and 10-year bonds fell six basis points each to 3.61% and 3.93%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds also fell six basis points to 4.41%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps tracked bond yields lower.\par The seven-year swap offer fell six basis points to 3.75%, and the\par 10-year swap offer ended eight basis points lower at 4.08%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 212 basis points\par from 214 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interbank rates mostly stayed steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.5625% 0.6250%   0.6875% 0.8125%\par 3-Month           0.8125% 0.9375%   0.8125% 0.9375%\par 1-Year            1.1875% 1.3125%   1.1875% 1.3125%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-152;\par Amit.Prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020425dy4p000t9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
