\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Jitters Grow \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2002\par 11:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Tuesday, as the inexorable rise of several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-month highs in recent sessions heightened jitters about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Still, except for Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to curb any excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility - there wasn't any solid evidence that the other monetary authorities in Asia had bought U.S. dollars to check the appreciation of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  DBS Bank said in a research note that the region's central banks "appeared to have relaxed their rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance" due to growing confidence over exports, easing worries about a tottering yen and the cost-push pressures on inflation as a result of higher oil prices. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, we believe that central banks want to keep domestic interest rates low for as long as possible to put the recovery on a sound footing," said DBS Bank's market strategist, Philip Wee, \par \par \uc2  "Growing official talk of improving exports and capital inflows suggest that policymakers may not mind modest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which would not only help curb imported inflation but also be useful in reflecting the recent improvement in investor confidence," he added. \par \par \uc2  But Wee and other analysts warned that the yen remains the wild card - even though the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to extend its recent uptrend in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "The market trend is fairly bullish for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but there's always the yen risk," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  For now, however, Mohi-uddin expects the yen to strengthen further toward Y128.50 against the dollar, and in turn continue to bolster the rest of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0905 GMT (5:05 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y129.95, marginally higher than Y129.89 late Monday in New York, but lower than Y130.08 late Monday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bounce off its morning low of Y129.76 in Asia Tuesday helped lift the U.S. dollar off its intraday lows against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  "It shows that the market is a bit short of U.S. dollars against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the yen is still important," said Mohi-uddin at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar lost ground after hitting a fresh six-week high of around S$1.8120 in early trade on aggressive U.S. dollar sales by U.S. banks and Hong Kong-based funds. \par \par \uc2  A large Singapore bank had bought U.S. dollars at the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intraday lows, giving some semblance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although some dealers said this was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Technically, observers said it made sense to pick up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that level, with the 200-day moving average - which acts as critical support - hovering around S$1.8122 on the charts. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the U.S. dollar was higher at S$1.8191, compared with S$1.8177 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea also contributed to the won's slight pullback from its intraday high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Still, the won ended stronger at a three-month peak of KRW1,306.3 against the dollar, compared with KRW1,308.8 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar closed at a four-month high, even after relinquishing the bulk of its earlier gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - buying an estimated US$200 million in the market - and banks covered their short positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sales by exporters and foreign equity fund inflows helped shore up the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar closed at NT$34.810 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stronger compared with NT$34.845 Monday. The New Taiwan dollar hit an intraday high of NT$34.760. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume for the session totaled US$695 million, nearly half of Monday's US$1.201 billion, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  discouraged \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  participants. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the dollar closed at 50.960 pesos, little changed from PHP50.970 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said Tuesday the bank's closely watched overnight rates may have already bottomed, but added that monetary authorities can hold the rates steady until November. \par \par \uc2  Buenaventura is trying to dispel market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon tighten monetary policy due to rising inflationary pressure. Inflation in March rose to 3.6% on year after falling to a two-year low of 3.4% in February. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was weaker at 9,435 rupiah to the dollar, compared with IDR9,255 late Monday, on short-covering in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was indifferent to Standard & Poor's as-expected downgrade of Indonesia's sovereign ratings to "selective default," as it was a technical move following the government's recent debt rescheduling with the Paris Club of official creditors. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slightly weaker at 43.360 baht to the dollar, compared with THB43.260 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The baht shrugged off the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave its key 14-day repurchase rate unchanged at 2% at its regular monetary policy meeting Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020423dy4n000qu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
