\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Hits 6-Week High On Regional Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2002\par 10:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar rose to a six-week high against the U.S. dollar late Monday, as U.S. investment houses and local banks sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following its decline against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was quite heavy. The local dollar retreated slightly from its intraday high on fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it from rising too sharply, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  Around 0842 GMT (4:42 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8177, down from S$1.8221 late Friday. It traded between S$1.8160 and S$1.8220 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened steady against its Singapore counterpart, but began its descent in early trade as U.S. investment houses unwound long positions in the wake of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against other regional units. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks initially sought to stem the U.S. dollar's slide by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.8200, raising suspicion they may be acting on behalf of the MAS. However, in the face of persistent heavy selling by U.S. investment houses, the local banks changed track and turned sellers. \par \par \uc2  Heavy selling briefly pushed the U.S. dollar to a six-week low of S$1.8160 in mid-afternoon trading, but it bounced off the low amid fears the MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell too sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has maintained a range of S$1.8170 to S$1.8180 since. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar still looks heavy and could test initial support at S$1.8150 unless it reverses its slide against the yen. Stronger support for the U.S. unit is expected at S$1.8100. \par \par \uc2  They expect the U.S. dollar's upside to be capped at S$1.8230. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds mostly fell as market participants took profit from gains last week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said buying interest in four- and five-year interest rate swaps also exerted upward pressure on some bond yields, resulting in the bonds being sold. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were likely hedging ahead of an anticipated S$300 million 5-year issue by Singapore's Tuas Power Ltd., and a planned S$500 million issue by the Land Transport Authority. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was flat at 1.64%, while the yield on five-year bonds rose three basis points to 3.08%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bond was up three basis points at 3.68%, while the yield on 10-year bonds rose four basis points at 4.01%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was up three basis points at 4.49%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer rose five basis points to 3.84%. The 10-year swap offer ended 5\par basis points higher at 4.18.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 218 basis points\par from 216 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were steady amid ample liquidity in\par the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.8750% 1.0000%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.8750% 1.3125%   1.1875% 1.3125%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-152;\par Amit.Prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020422dy4m000mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
