\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Hits 5-Wk High As Regionals Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2002\par 10:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar hit a five-week high against the U.S. dollar Friday before partially surrendering its intraday gains as market participants squared positions ahead of a meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the group of seven industrialized nations over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Around 0835 GMT (4:35 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8221, down from S$1.8250 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.8188 and S$1.8240 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened slightly lower against its Singapore counterpart, but fell steadily until mid-afternoon on persistent selling by U.S. investment houses and European banks in line with its broader weakness against the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's decline below Y130 pushed it below the key level of S$1.8220, which triggered stop-loss selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Singapore dollar. Another set of stop-losses were triggered when the U.S. dollar fell below S$1.8200, pushing it to its intraday low of S$1.8188, its lowest level since mid-March, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar bounced back above S$1.8200 as its sharp decline prompted market participants to cover their short positions amid fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp slide. \par \par \uc2  Squaring of U.S. dollar positions against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the group of seven industrialized nations over the weekend also helped the dollar regain some lost ground against the yen, Singapore dollar and other Asian units. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar's upside to be capped by profit-taking on moves toward S$1.8250, while MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and potential corporate buying will likely support it initially at S$1.8180 and then at S$1.8150. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds rose in a day of active trading as market participants bought bonds on expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve may put off rate hikes until August, and domestic rates in Singapore would remain weak though the year. \par \par \uc2  Participants also bought bonds ahead of two large issues maturing next month. Dealers said S$2.50 billion worth of one-year treasury bills mature May 14, followed by S$750 million worth of five-year bonds May 15. \par \par \uc2  The one-year bills likely will be replaced by a new one-year issue scheduled for May 2, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, participants were covering for an expected shortfall arising from the maturity of the five-year bonds. The next bond issue won't take place until July 1, when the government issues new 10-year paper. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down four basis points at 1.63%, while the yield on five-year bonds slipped three basis points to 3.05%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was four basis points lower at 3.64%, while the yield on 10-year bonds was down one basis point at 3.98%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds slipped one basis point to 4.47%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, lower bond yields triggered paying\par interest which pushed swaps lower, dealers said. The most liquid\par seven-year swap offer slipped three basis points to 3.79%. The 10-year\par swap offer also ended three basis points lower at 4.13.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 216 basis points\par from 214 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were steady amid ample liquidity in\par the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.7500% 0.8750%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par 3-Month           0.7500% 0.8750%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par 1-Year            1.8750% 1.3125%   1.1875% 1.3125%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-152;\par Amit.Prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020419dy4j000ow\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
