\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Rupiah Hovers Near 6-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2002\par 12:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stronger Monday with the rupiah continuing to hover near a six-month high as investors bet Indonesia's creditors will agree to lenient terms this week for the country's debt rescheduling. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, South Korean won and New Taiwan tracked a stronger yen, while financial markets in Thailand and the Philippines were closed for public holidays. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah strengthened to near a key level of IDR9,500 per dollar early in the session amid optimism that the government will be given some breathing room on debt repayment and build on recent successes in its privatization plans. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading the dollar was quoted at IDR9,525 down from IDR9,560 at Friday's close. It was the rupiah's best performance since last September. \par \par \uc2  An agreement by the Paris Club group of creditor nations to roll over an estimated $5.5 billion in Indonesian debt maturing between now and 2003 would also clear the way for the International Monetary Fund to press ahead with its Indonesian aid package. The creditors are slated to meet Thursday and Friday in the French capital. \par \par \uc2  For the first time, creditors are likely to allow Indonesia to delay repayment of interest on its debt, as well as principal. In two previous Paris Club reschedulings, creditors only agreed to push back principal payment deadlines. \par \par \uc2  "The debt rescheduling is key for Indonesia's projected 2.5% (of GDP) deficit target compared to 3.7% last year," Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah garnered additional support from comments by visiting U.S. Trade Representative Robert Zoellick over the weekend expressing his support for President Megawati Sukarnoputri's efforts to reform the economy and help in the global fight against terrorism. \par \par \uc2  The won benefited from the yen's rise, despite heavy demand for dollars by foreigners who recently sold shares in the South Korean stock market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,328.2 won, down from Thursday's close of KRW1,330.5. Markets in Seoul were closed Friday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose against the dollar Monday as uncertainty about how events in the Middle East would unfold prompted some players to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was at Y131.27, below Y131.65 in New York late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners snapped up dollars against the won Monday after being net sellers of local shares for four straight sessions. On Monday, they unloaded KRW263.3 billion in shares, pulling down the main stock index by 1.9% to 900.69. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar strengthened against the U.S. dollar to its best finish in more than three weeks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.978, down from NT$34.999 Thursday. Dealings were valued at US$534 million, down from US$629 million Thursday. The market was closed for a public holiday Friday. \par \par \uc2  The local dollar's rise was slowed by the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which bought more than US$200 million in the session, mostly in the final minutes of trading. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive volatility in the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's rise was tempered by concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be reluctant to allow it to appreciate to sharply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8349, down from S$1.8358 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  News that Dutch electronics giant Philips Electronics will move its regional headquarters from Singapore to Hong Kong, caused worries over Singapore's competitiveness to resurface. That, in turn, prompted concerns among market players that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local dollar competitive against its regional peers. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020408dy48000sh\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (Singapore near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx